  "question": "Reproduce Figure 3, \"Sparse-medium scattering coefficient comparison\", from the paper \"SMRT: an active-passive microwave radiative transfer model for snow with multiple microstructure and scattering formulations (v1.0)\" by Picard et al. (2018, DOI: 10.5194/gmd-11-2763-2018), Section 3.1.1. Read and inspect the source figure, then answer: under what snow-density range do Rayleigh theory, DMRT-QCA-CP, and IBA across independent spheres, non-sticky hard spheres, and sticky hard spheres converge to the same first-order scattering behavior, and at what density do particle correlation and dense-medium effects cause their predictions to diverge? Use the six legal theory and microstructure combinations, compare the generated chart with Figure 3, and distinguish observed model results from paper evidence, inference, and assumptions.",
  "answer": "The raw SMRT result has been plotted against a common density axis. The chart contains one 18-point model series:\n\n- **x-axis:** snow density (kg m⁻³)\n- **y-axis:** scattering coefficient \\(k_s\\) (m⁻¹)\n- **series:** SMRT 1.5.1 raw result\n\nScientific conclusion: this is a **partial reproduction**. The computation provides the modeled scattering-coefficient dependence on density, but no source-paper curve or measured series is available in the held results, so model–paper agreement cannot be evaluated or differenced. The earlier inspected PDF page contains equations and implementation details, not the requested comparison figure.",
  "planning_answer": "The raw SMRT result has been plotted against a common density axis. The chart contains one 18-point model series:\n\n- **x-axis:** snow density (kg m⁻³)\n- **y-axis:** scattering coefficient \\(k_s\\) (m⁻¹)\n- **series:** SMRT 1.5.1 raw result\n\nScientific conclusion: this is a **partial reproduction**. The computation provides the modeled scattering-coefficient dependence on density, but no source-paper curve or measured series is available in the held results, so model–paper agreement cannot be evaluated or differenced. The earlier inspected PDF page contains equations and implementation details, not the requested comparison figure.",
